<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shōgun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seii Taishogun, shogun, shogunate, shogunal, Barbarian-Subduing Generalissimo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shōgun was the title of the military rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, short for Seii Taishōgun, meaning barbarian-subduing generalissimo. Shoguns held real power while the emperor retained ceremonial authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> ruled Japan from the early seventeenth century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, when the shogunate was abolished and power returned to the emperor, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> note.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shogun.jpg" alt="Shōgun" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Takanobu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minamoto_no_Yoritomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shōgun is short for sei-i taishōgun, a title meaning commander-in-chief of the expeditionary force against the barbarians. The barbarians in question were the Emishi, tribes of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> who resisted the governance of the imperial court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> the title was given to commanders for the length of a campaign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakanoue-no-tamuramaro">Sakanoue no Tamuramaro</a> (758–811), who brought the Emishi lands of Mutsu and Dewa under the Yamato state, is generally recognised as the first shogun, although sources also name Ōtomo no Otomaro and Tajihi no Agatamori. In this early period shoguns held no real political power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a> controlled the court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> gained ascendancy over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1185, the title was revived to regularise his position. He established a system based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> gained some political powers, while the emperor and aristocracy remained the rulers in law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-toba">Emperor Go-Toba</a> awarded him the title in 1192. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, the family of Yoritomo's wife, then took power from the Kamakura shoguns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> was awarded the title in 1338 and established the Ashikaga shogunate, which nominally lasted until 1573.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> was given the title in 1603 and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> at Edo, now Tokyo. Fifteen Tokugawa shoguns ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, which ended in 1867 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> relinquished the office to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The shogun's officials were known as the bakufu, tent government, a term that recalled the shogun's role as field commander and suggested an office meant to be temporary. The shogun held civil, military, diplomatic and judicial authority and in theory served the emperor, who acted as chief priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>. No shogun tried to usurp the throne, partly because the shogun's authority was held to come from the emperor and partly because of a tradition that traced the imperial line from the age of the gods. During parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku periods</a> the shoguns were figureheads. The shogunate lasted nearly 700 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shogun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1180 to 1185 the Genpei War between the Taira and Minamoto clans ended with the victory of Minamoto no Yoritomo and the founding of the Kamakura <b>shogunate</b>, later dominated by the Hōjō clan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians disagree about the year the <b>shogunate</b> began.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through it Satsuma gained access to Chinese goods and information, while Ryukyuan embassies travelled to the <b>shoguns</b> at Edo between 1634 and 1850, dressed in Chinese style to display the reach of Tokugawa authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tokugawa Shogunate, or Tokugawa bakufu, was established in 1603 when the imperial court in Kyoto appointed Tokugawa Ieyasu <b>Sei-i Taishogun</b> ('barbarian-subduing generalissimo').</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Japan Confucian ideas arrived with Chinese writing and learning by the 6th century; under the Tokugawa shogunate the Neo-Confucianism of Zhu Xi became the official teaching of the <b>shogunate</b>’s academy and was taught in schools for the samurai.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…sun, the wind and thunder; mountains such as Fuji and Miwa, waterfalls, rocks and ancient trees; the ancestors of clans and families; and outstanding human beings, such as the scholar Sugawara no Michizane, worshipped as Tenjin, the kami of learning, and the <b>shogun</b> Tokugawa Ieyasu, enshrined at Nikko.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
